  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1125 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAloft, the flow is primarily zonal with a slight southeastward tilt. \nHigh pressure sits above the CWA with a subtle low pressure over \nTexas. With the low being too weak and far south to tangibly impact \nthe forecast area, the patter of seasonably warm and dry \ntemperatures will continue. Highs into Monday will continue to hover \nin the mid-80s and potentially push 90 degrees near the end. No \nprecipitation chances are present in the forecast. The only notable \nweather forecast in this 4 day stretch is stronger winds into this \nweekend. Friday will be the stronger south/southwest winds at 20-25 \nmph and gusts up to 35 mph especially out west. Saturday and Sunday \nwill see similar winds albeit 5 mph or so stronger. The silver \nlining from a weather perspective is that relative humidities are \nexpected to stay above 25% and quell most of the fire weather risk. \nThat said, take traditional fire weather precautions including extra \ncare with ignition sources. \n\nThe focus continues to be on the pattern shift early next week. \nEnsembles still have a trough developing off of the West Coast into \nMonday. Current long-ranged models show scattered storms \npotentially, especially near the Oklahoma border. Behind these \nstorms, models also show a more widespread round of storms behind \nthe initial one into Wednesday. The severity of these storms are \nstill up in the air, but can not be ruled out at this stage. \nEnsembles have remained steady with run to run continuity at \naround 0.25-0.5\" of rain across those two days. This should be \ntreated an a very preliminary estimate with a ton of \nuncertainty until the system is better resolved. \n\nOutside of the precipitation, the temperatures will drop through \nnext week. Tuesday highs will drop to the lower 80s and slowly \ndecreasing by the day until the highs on Friday in the 60s. No other \nactive weather is expected through the forecast period other than \nthe precipitation.",
